    "reasoning_validity": {
      "pass": true,
      "findings": [
        {
          "gate": "reasoning_validity",
          "severity": "minor",
          "source": "reviewer",
          "detail": "D1 [CL3; counterarguments item 3] Draft 2 adds a fallback for the case where v3 can't be confirmed before the trade: execute EUR 4M now and the remaining EUR 2M once v3 is confirmed. Review-1 D5 proposed this, so the draft is not at fault for including it, but the review's suggestion was incomplete. If v3 is in force, holding back EUR 2M is itself a hedge below 75%. Under s.4.3 that is a deviation needing Audit Committee approval, and the draft never says so. It also never weighs the opposite error. If v1 turned out to govern, hedging EUR 6M would be an over-hedge, and whether that breaches v1 is unknown. The draft calls the delay 'a temporary under-hedge' and says it 'limits the risk', without showing that it does. It should either record the interim under-hedge as a deviation (for example by getting quick Audit Committee sign-off or confirmation) or explain why under-hedging is the safer error."
        },
        {
          "gate": "reasoning_validity",
          "severity": "minor",
          "source": "reviewer",
          "detail": "D3 [criteriaAssessments C4; CL16; K21 vs K6] The new C4 scenarios centre future spot on 1.12 (random walk), but the hedged leg is valued at the 1.1291 parity forward. At the centre, hedged proceeds (K21, USD 9,014,620) therefore exceed unhedged (K6, USD 8,960,000) by about USD 54.6k. The draft doesn't explain this. A reader could take it as hedging having a positive expected payoff, when the gap is just the forward carry and the choice of centring convention. If the distribution were centred on the forward instead, the two centres would be equal. The draft should state this, or show both centrings."
        }
      ]
    },
